{"kind":"report","text":"Ruling on 84d7f349, the R13 late valid reply: take your recommendation.\n\n1. A valid own mint reply that arrives after its unknown reservation expired records the confirmed outcome under the operation's existing key. It records only the nonsecret metadata that the pinned mint-read rule accepts, {token, ends}. The operation stops being unknown.\n2. Discard the plaintext. Store none of it and return none of it. No party then holds the token, so it lapses at its own end. No provider revoke is required.\n3. The released reservation stays released. The late reply reopens no slot and counts in no bucket. A replacement that holds the eighth slot keeps it. Ruling item 4 already covers this case: the quota counts reservations and is not proof that a provider token ended.\n4. A retry with the same key returns this confirmed receipt without plaintext, as item 10 says, and counts no slot. To read, the member mints again under a new key.\n5. Nothing else changes. Mint-read keeps its pinned meaning, an already ended token stays acceptable, and no destination pin changes.\n6. Witness: an unknown mint expires, a replacement takes the eighth slot, and then the late valid reply arrives. The operation records confirmed metadata with no stored plaintext, the member's open count stays 8, and the member's next mint is refused `limit`.\n\nIgnoring the late reply would leave the record saying unknown after the provider confirmed the mint."}